A||||11||KevinP||anybody ever buy a police impound?||||5||
Z||000000||KevinP||05-15-2001||01:02 PM||||looking for a new car, just wondering if anyone has ever bought from one of those police impound auctions.  I've heard of some people getting some real good deals, so I'm curious what everyone's experience with them is.  Thanks,<P>Kevin<BR>'84 SE about to go up for parts||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||05-15-2001||05:29 PM||||I picked up an '86 Olds Cutlass (full size) with just over 85,000 miles on it a couple of years ago. It was an old detective car and was bumped on the front. I gave $600 for it, then spent a few hundred fixing and painting it. It had been grossly over maintained! New everything- shocks, axle bearings, brakes, belts, hoses, carb, A/C system, headliner, tires and exhaust. There are some good deals to be had at these auctions.||||reg||1
Z||000002||onfire||05-16-2001||02:37 AM||||I bought an 84 GMC S15 extended cab 4x4 from an auction. For $800. It is currently for sale for $2250. How's that for a good deal? When I sell it half of the money is going towards an 85 GT I am looking at, and the other half I am taking back to the auction, to buy another car.<P>At most places they will let you start the vehicle, and even open the hood. If someone that works their is with you. They don't want anybody messing with the cars before they sell them. Definately go to the preview usually before the auction starts. Pick about 4 or 5 cars you want to bid on, because you won't necassarily get the first one. Make sure to leave yourself enough cash to pay for taxes, license, and if there is a buyers premium, usually not more than 10%.<BR>Feel free to ask more questions.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||05-16-2001||09:35 AM||||So where would one look for info on times and locations of local auctions?  Are they publicly advertised, or do you just need to know where to look?||||reg||1
Z||000004||p4n1c||05-16-2001||09:51 AM||||My dad's first car was an old caprice or crown vic, can't remember which one, had police everything on it.  Handled like a pig, but moved like snot.  He paid $400 for it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4 OD auto<BR>"Ride the geriatric mobile.. err..  rocket"||||reg||1
Z||000005||thomas_l||05-16-2001||10:14 AM||||Just beware of a clear title. That can really muck things up when dealing with a police auction (unless you're bidding on a cop car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">). Most regular auctions give you a little protection by requiring sellers to either show a clear title or post a bond.||||reg||1
Z||000006||onfire||05-16-2001||01:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>So where would one look for info on times and locations of local auctions?  Are they publicly advertised, or do you just need to know where to look?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Some of the auctions in my area are advertised in the news paper. Usually in the classified section. Also check out the little nickel. And once you go to your first auction get on the mailing list. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||jerpro||05-16-2001||05:18 PM||||well guys. I have worked at a police impound and can tell you that all they have is junk. And the nice cars (the ones from the drug busts) are usuall on hold by the police for months and then the owner comes and gets them.<BR>All impounded cars are going to have damage to them because they have to be searched and "cleaned" before the auction. And if there are no keys (wich is the majority of the cases) and you have to search the truck of the vehicle well..... get the hammer out and pry it. (it was a great strees reliever when I worked there!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )<BR>ONe good thing is that no matter how bad the car was or what history it had, the title will get to your hands as "clean". It's prob. the best way to get a salvagable car with a "clean" title.<BR>Any more questions on auctions and I could help you guys out.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jay||05-16-2001||05:26 PM||||So then when do the Ferraris come up for auction?  It Toronto last year the Police force sold almost all of it's fleet of Harley Motorcycles to get new ones.  They also sold cars, jeeps, typewriters, desks you name it they sold it.  I guess they were upgrading to new stuff!<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande||||reg||1
Z||000009||RNGCRSH||05-17-2001||12:43 AM||||My 87 GT, came from a police aution. bad interior, no keys, broken ignition, and a clean title.  Best of all no body damage and runs/drives excelent.||||reg||1
Z||000010||onfire||05-17-2001||04:06 AM||||Their are a few places I have gone to. One of them doesn't do anything to the cars. They might search the car for drugs or a dead body but they don't clean them out. The other place I go to gets all the police cars and some of the real nice cars. The website is here. <A HREF="http://www.murphyauction.com/embezzle.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.murphyauction.com/embezzle.html</A> <BR>The murphy autions get kinda high priced. Alot of people get caught up in the moment, and over bid.||||reg||1
Z||000011||onfire||05-17-2001||04:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>So where would one look for info on times and locations of local auctions?  Are they publicly advertised, or do you just need to know where to look?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <A HREF="http://www.delawareautoexchange.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.delawareautoexchange.com/</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.auctionweb.com/aanc/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.auctionweb.com/aanc/</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.auction.net/north_carolina.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.auction.net/north_carolina.htm</A> ||||reg||1
